markdown <<MARKDOWN
# List of Objects

#### To get comfortable working with data in JavaScript, it helps to understand how information is organized when we store multiple related items. This is done with an **array of objects**.

#### In the example below, **supplies is an array**, meaning it’s a list that holds multiple items.

#### Each of those items is an **object**, and every object represents one record. In this case, one classroom supply.
---

<img src="https://images.code.org/c5a7e0009b56ddb7354fac9fdbf65bf8-image-1766003842988.png" alt="example array of objects" width="750px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

## How to interpret this structure:

- #### The array (`supplies`) is the entire dataset — a list of all supply records.

- #### Each object inside the array represents a single supply item.

- #### The object’s properties (`id`, `name`, `location`) describe different pieces of information about that item.

- #### For example,

	- #### `{ id: 103, name: "Stapler", location: "Desk 1" }` tells us that the item with ID 3 is a Stapler, stored in Desk 1.

#### This structure is extremely common in real-world data: a dataset (the array) made up of many individual records (the objects), each with fields that describe them.


---

## 🔍 Check for Understanding

> #### Before moving on, pause and define the following terms in your own words: **array, object, and property**.
> - #### How do these three concepts work together when organizing data? 

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
Check your thinking
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px; background:#f7f8fa;">
<p style = "font-size: 14pt;">An <b>array</b> is a collection or list that holds multiple items.</p>
<p style = "font-size: 14pt;">An <b>object</b> is a single item in that list that stores related information.</p>
<p style = "font-size: 14pt;">A <b>property</b> is a specific piece of information (a key-value pair) inside an object.</p>
<p style = "font-size: 14pt;">They work together because an array stores multiple objects, and each object uses properties to describe its data.</p>
</div>
</details>


---

## Next up

#### We will use AI to generate an array with 5 objects and practice updating an app. 




MARKDOWN
